  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is equal to ${F}^{\u2032}(C).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $F(C)$ with respect to $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{\u2032}(C) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{9}{5}C+32) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the air temperature in degrees Fahrenheit with respect to the air temperature in degrees Celsius is $\\frac{9}{5}$ degrees Fahrenheit per degree of Celsius.",
